"""Source-specific transcription after all four main and four SI pages were read/viewed."""
TITLE='Precipitation of Nanocrystalline CeO2 Using Triethanolamine'
AUTHORS=['Ranjan K. Pati','Ivan C. Lee','Karen J. Gaskell','Sheryl H. Ehrman']
# id,role,page,locator,title,claim,specimen scope,quantities(meaning,raw,unit),flags,claim class
FACTS=[
('identity','main',1,'Title and journal heading','Source identity','Langmuir 2009, volume 25, pages 67–70; DOI10.1021/la8031286. Received September24,2008; revised November21,2008; published on Web December5,2008.','paper',[],'','bibliographic'),
('abstract','main',1,'Abstract','Study scope','Precipitation with triethanolamine uses the precursor-associated molecular water. The authors associate small as-prepared crystallites with limited water and solvent-dependent particle dispersity with alcohol chain length.','study',[('abstract crystallite diameter','3','nm')],'C2','reported_summary'),
('applications','main',1,'Introduction paragraph1','Prior ceria applications','Prior work motivates ceria for solid oxide fuel cells, water gas shift catalysis, polishing, sensors, UV absorption, hybrid solar cells and H2S sorption. Oxygen storage/ionic conductivity and size effects are cited context, not measurements in this study.','cited literature',[],'G8','literature_context'),
('prior-routes','main',1,'Introduction paragraphs2–3','Prior routes and agglomeration','Aqueous precipitation, hydrothermal, alkoxide, combustion and electrochemical routes are discussed. Hydrogen-bond bridging and drying capillary forces are cited explanations for hard agglomerates; alkoxide powders are described as more reactive toward densification.','cited literature',[],'G8','literature_context'),
('prior-diethylamine','main',1,'Introduction right column','Cited diethylamine comparison','Li et al. reference19 used diethylamine and precursor-associated water; pure cubic ceria was reported after calcination. This earlier procedure was not supplied or inspected.','cited comparison',[('cited calcination temperature','400','degC'),('cited calcination duration','2','h'),('cited crystallite diameter','~8','nm')],'G8','literature_context'),
('tea-motivation','main',1,'Introduction right column','TEA motivation','The authors propose that TEA both releases hydroxide from molecular water and lowers oxide formation temperature; its possible polymeric cerium complex is motivated by cited literature.','author rationale',[],'G8','author_interpretation'),
('cerium-material','main',1,'Experimental first paragraph','Cerium precursor','Cerium nitrate hexahydrate, Ce(NO3)3·6H2O, Aldrich Chemicals USA, is the cerium source.','all three solvent preparations',[('cerium nitrate purity','99','%'),('waters per hydrate formula','6','formula_count')],'','reported_material'),
('tea-material','main',1,'Experimental first paragraph','Triethanolamine identity','Triethanolamine is supplied by Aldrich Chemicals USA. The paper prints (C2H5OH)3N; this literal formula is retained as a source inconsistency, not silently used to define a chemical graph.','all three solvent preparations',[('TEA purity','98','%')],'C1','reported_material'),
('alcohol-materials','main',1,'Experimental first paragraph','Alcohol solvents','Anhydrous ethanol,1-propanol and1-butanol are Fisher Chemicals solvents. The anhydrous qualifier is explicitly attached to ethanol; do not automatically attach it to the other alcohols.','separate solvent variants',[('ethanol purity','99.9','%'),('1-propanol purity','99.9','%'),('1-butanol purity','99.9','%')],'','reported_material'),
('stocks','main',1,'Experimental stock preparation','Six separate stock formulations','Dissolve cerium nitrate hexahydrate and TEA separately in each of the three alcohol solvents. Reported concentrations are final stock concentrations; masses, preparation volumes and storage are not reported.','three nitrate stocks and three TEA stocks',[('cerium nitrate stock concentration','0.1','M'),('TEA stock concentration','0.4','M')],'G1','reported_preparation'),
('precipitation','main',1,'Experimental last paragraph continued mainp2','Room-temperature precipitation','At room temperature, drip cerium nitrate solution from a burette into TEA solution in an Erlenmeyer flask under mild stirring. Use the same selected alcohol for both stocks.','each solvent-specific route',[('nitrate stock transferred volume','100','mL'),('nitrate transferred concentration','0.1','M'),('addition rate','3–4','mL/min'),('TEA receiving volume','100','mL'),('TEA receiving concentration','0.4','M')],'G1','reported_operation'),
('poststir','main',2,'Experimental first paragraph','Post-precipitation stirring','Continue stirring for1h after completion of precipitation. Do not substitute the calculated dripping time for this hold.','each solvent-specific route',[('post-precipitation stirring duration','1','h')],'G2','reported_operation'),
('filtration','main',2,'Experimental first paragraph','Suction filtration','Filter with a suction filter and Whatman42 paper. The precipitate is retained for washing; the filtrate supplies the completeness check.','each route precipitate and separate filtrate',[('filter pore diameter','2.5','um')],'G2','reported_operation'),
('filtrate-control','main',2,'Experimental first paragraph','Completeness check','Add a10mL filtrate aliquot with ammonium hydroxide solution to confirm precipitation completeness. Ammonium hydroxide amount/concentration and an explicit visual pass criterion are unreported; it is not the main synthesis precipitant.','filtrate diagnostic aliquot',[('filtrate aliquot','10','mL')],'G2','reported_control'),
('alcohol-wash-dry','main',2,'Experimental first paragraph','Alcohol wash and ambient drying','Wash the retained precipitate with its corresponding solvent alcohol before drying at room temperature for24h. Wash volume/cycles and numeric room temperature are unreported.','each retained precipitate',[('ambient drying duration','24','h')],'G2','reported_operation'),
('acetone-wash','main',2,'Experimental first paragraph','Acetone wash after ambient drying','Wash resulting powder with acetone to remove remaining alcohol, unreacted TEA and other byproduct. A further drying operation or its conditions are not specified.','each dried powder',[ ],'G2','reported_operation'),
('calcination','main',2,'Experimental first paragraph','Thermal post-treatment','Heat as-prepared material at10°C/min and calcine at200°C for3h. The synthesis-calcination atmosphere, cooling program and loading are not stated; the air condition belongs to TGA only.','post-treatment of each preparation',[('calcination ramp rate','10','degC/min'),('calcination temperature','200','degC'),('calcination duration','3','h')],'G3','reported_operation'),
('xrd-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Powder diffraction','Characterize as-prepared and calcined powders with Bruker AXS D8 Advance using Cu Kα incident radiation. Crystallite diameter is estimated with the cited Scherrer equation; no explicit equation or shape factor is supplied.','XRD specimens; individual physical batches unknown',[('Cu Kalpha wavelength','1.5408','angstrom')],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('bet-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Surface area','BET gas absorption, as worded in the source, uses Micromeritics ASAP2010C. Later interpretation refers to nitrogen absorption; sample degassing, gas grade and isotherm are unreported.','calcined powders for reported areas',[],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('tem-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Microscopy','JEOL2100 HRTEM is used for morphology and crystallite size; source Figure1 includes actual selected-area diffraction. Grid preparation and operating voltage are not supplied.','Figure1 samples',[],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('tga-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Thermogravimetry','Perkin-Elmer TGA-7 measurement is performed in air at10°C/min to identify impurities.','as-prepared thermal sample',[('TGA heating rate','10','degC/min')],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('dsc-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Calorimetry','TA Instruments2920 Modulated DSC examines as-prepared thermal behavior; Figure3 also includes a calcined comparison. Modulation, atmosphere and heating rate for DSC are not explicitly stated.','Figure3 DSC states',[],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('xps-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Photoelectron spectroscopy','Kratos AXIS165 uses Al Kα in hybrid mode; survey and high-resolution pass energies are distinct. The sampled surface composition is not an independently measured whole-powder formula.','as-prepared and calcined XPS states',[('Al Kalpha photon energy','1486.6','eV'),('survey pass energy','160','eV'),('high-resolution pass energy','20','eV')],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('dls-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Dynamic light scattering','Solution particle distributions use a He–Ne laser05-LHP-121, Melles Griot, and FEU-79 photon counter. Dispersion preparation, concentration, viscosity model and temperature are unreported.','solution particles in three solvents',[],'C2 G4','reported_acquisition'),
('packing-acquisition','main',2,'Experimental characterization','Packing-density estimate','Hand-pack powder into a preweighed1mL pipet and weigh pipet plus powder. This is a rough bulk packing-density estimate, not a measured crystal density.','room-temperature-dried porous precipitates',[('pipet nominal volume','1','mL')],'G4','reported_acquisition'),
('colors','main',2,'Results opening','Color evolution','Colorless solution becomes yellow, then light khaki yellow as precipitate thickens. Ligand-field absorption, initial hydroxide and final CeO2 formation are the authors’ assignments; color alone does not resolve the as-prepared mixture.','precipitation trajectory',[],'C3','reported_observation_and_interpretation'),
('tem-size','main',2,'Results Figure1a–c','As-prepared crystallites','Individual grains are called crystallites and their aggregates particles. All three as-prepared TEM samples show aggregated crystallites with average diameter3nm and standard deviation1nm; no significant solvent effect on crystallite size is asserted.','ethanol/propanol/butanol as-prepared TEM',[('mean crystallite diameter','3','nm'),('crystallite diameter standard deviation','1','nm')],'C3','reported_measurement'),
('saed','main',2,'Results Figure1a–c and embedded patterns','As-prepared SAED','As-prepared patterns are assigned {200},{220},{311}. Measured dominant HRTEM interfringe distance0.267nm is compared with cubic-CeO2 {200} reference spacing0.271nm. This local microscopy evidence coexists with unresolved bulk as-prepared XRD.','Figure1a–c',[('observed lattice fringe distance','0.267','nm'),('cubic CeO2 reference {200} spacing','0.271','nm')],'C3','reported_structure'),
('figure1-bars','main',2,'Figure1 four scale bars and SAED labels','Original microscopy annotations','All four HRTEM panels have5nm scale bars. The calcined 1-butanol panel(d) additionally labels111, while a–c label200,220,311. No numerical lattice parameter or atom coordinates are given.','Figure1 panelsa–d',[('panel a scale bar','5','nm'),('panel b scale bar','5','nm'),('panel c scale bar','5','nm'),('panel d scale bar','5','nm')],'G5','figure_annotation'),
('dls-values','main',2,'Results DLS paragraph','DLS size comparison','The Results call the quantities average hydrodynamic radii: ethanol72nm(SD20),1-propanol50nm(SD18),1-butanol14nm(SD8). The Conclusion instead calls the same values diameters. Both definitions remain explicit; no factor-of-two correction is imposed.','three solvent solution-particle contexts',[('ethanol reported hydrodynamic value','72','nm'),('ethanol SD','20','nm'),('propanol reported hydrodynamic value','50','nm'),('propanol SD','18','nm'),('butanol reported hydrodynamic value','14','nm'),('butanol SD','8','nm')],'C2','reported_measurement'),
('dls-literature','main',2,'Results DLS comparison','Cited agglomeration comparison','Chavan et al.23 reported yttria-doped ceria agglomerates1.5µm with6nm crystallites; this is a different material and prior experiment.','cited yttria-doped ceria',[('cited agglomerate size','1.5','um'),('cited crystallite size','6','nm')],'G8','literature_context'),
('solvent-effect','main',2,'Results right column','Solvent interpretation','DLS indicates increasing particle dispersion with alcohol chain length. The authors discuss low-concentration water affinity and increased steric inhibition of crystallite approach; cited Al(OH)3 work is context, not this material.','author interpretation of three solvents',[],'C2','author_interpretation'),
('calcined-tem','main',2,'Figure1d caption and text','Calcined microscopy state','Figure1d is1-butanol-derived material after200°C/3h calcination, assigned crystalline cubic CeO2 and compared with Figure2b.','butanol-calcined microscopy',[('figure1d calcination temperature','200','degC'),('figure1d calcination duration','3','h')],'G4','reported_structure'),
('asprep-xrd','main',2,'Results final paragraph','Unresolved as-prepared diffraction','As-prepared material is described as crystalline, but its pattern matches no known cerium compound in the database. Similarity to the cited La(TEA)2 nitrate complex motivates a possible Ce analogue and a possible mixture with Ce(OH)4 and hydrated CeO2. These are proposed identities, not solved product coordinates or quantitative mixture fractions.','as-prepared XRD specimen; solvent unspecified',[],'C3 G5','reported_structure_and_interpretation'),
('calcined-xrd','main',3,'Results first left paragraph; Figure2','Calcined diffraction and size','After200°C/3h the source assigns pure cubic CeO2. Scherrer diameter is approximately5nm, similar to calcined TEM. As-prepared diameter could not be estimated because no distinct appropriate XRD peak was observed. Lower formation temperature is attributed to TEA-related heat release.','calcined XRD specimen; solvent not explicit',[('Scherrer crystallite diameter','~5','nm')],'C3 G4','reported_structure'),
('bet-results','main',3,'Results left column','BET results','Calcined specific surface areas are78,80,84m²/g for ethanol,1-propanol,1-butanol respectively.','three calcined solvent powders',[('ethanol BET area','78','m2/g'),('propanol BET area','80','m2/g'),('butanol BET area','84','m2/g')],'','reported_measurement'),
('bet-diameters','main',3,'Results left column','Source-calculated BET diameters','Assuming spherical particles, the source calculates10,9.8,9.3nm for ethanol,1-propanol,1-butanol. They exceed crystallite diameters because not every surface in an aggregate is gas-accessible. No new diameter calculation is performed here.','three calcined solvent powders',[('ethanol source-calculated BET diameter','10','nm'),('propanol source-calculated BET diameter','9.8','nm'),('butanol source-calculated BET diameter','9.3','nm')],'','source_calculation'),
('tga-steps','main',3,'Results Figure3a discussion','Thermal mass loss','As-prepared1-butanol material loses mass in200–350°C and350–600°C intervals, assigned respectively to water/nitrate/TEA and carbon. No significant mass loss follows600°C, interpreted as impurity-free CeO2 at that stage. This is not the200°C synthesis product condition.','butanol as-prepared TGA',[('first loss interval','200–350','degC'),('second loss interval','350–600','degC'),('no further significant loss above','600','degC')],'G4','reported_measurement_and_interpretation'),
('tga-calculation','main',3,'Results thermal interpretation','Mixture inference from weight loss','Calculated conversion mass losses are about72% for proposed Ce–TEA nitrate complex and18% for Ce(OH)4, versus observed~58% total. The comparison supports a possible as-prepared mixture; do not solve unique phase fractions from it.','butanol as-prepared TGA/model',[('complex-to-CeO2 calculated loss','~72','%'),('hydroxide-to-CeO2 calculated loss','18','%'),('observed total loss','~58','%')],'C3','source_calculation_and_measurement'),
('dsc-results','main',3,'Results Figure3b–c discussion','Calorimetric transition','As-prepared sample shows an endothermic peak at173°C over40–250°C heating; calcined comparison has no such peak. The authors infer an irreversible CeO2-forming transition in150–180°C.','Figure3b as-prepared and3c calcined',[('endothermic peak','173','degC'),('DSC reported interval','40–250','degC'),('inferred transition interval','150–180','degC')],'G4','reported_measurement_and_interpretation'),
('xps-elements','main',3,'Results XPS paragraph','XPS element observations','As-prepared survey has Ce,O,N and some adventitious carbon; after200°C calcination nitrogen disappears while Ce,O and small carbon remain. No elemental atomic percentages are reported.','as-prepared/calcined XPS; solvent unspecified',[],'G4','reported_measurement'),
('xps-short-main','main',3,'Results XPS paragraph','Short-exposure oxidation states','Main states spectra collected under X-radiation for less than15min: as-prepared81%Ce3+/19%Ce4+, calcined33%Ce3+/67%Ce4+. SI uses~15min/15min wording; retain both.','short-exposure as-prepared and calcined surface states',[('main short exposure','<15','min'),('as-prepared CeIII fraction','81','%'),('as-prepared CeIV fraction','19','%'),('calcined CeIII fraction','33','%'),('calcined CeIV fraction','67','%')],'C4 C8','reported_measurement'),
('prior-deprotonation','main',3,'Discussion','Prior aqueous mechanism','Cited NH4OH/hexamethylenetetramine pathways describe Ce3+ oxidation, hydration/hydroxo complexes, slow deprotonation and dehydration with color changes. The cited deprotonation temperature70–85°C is not the current room-temperature TEA route.','literature mechanism',[('cited deprotonation temperature','70–85','degC')],'G8','literature_context'),
('hydroxo-conflict','main',3,'Discussion final paragraph continuedmainp4','Competing complex narratives','Absence of pinkish purple first motivates the expectation that Ce4+ forms no H2O/OH complex; the next page proposes simultaneous hydroxo and TEA complexes. Both author interpretations are retained as unresolved, not a proven sequence.','current-route mechanism',[],'C5','author_interpretation'),
('water-model','main',4,'Discussion first column','Precursor-associated water model','The authors compare four hydroxyl ions needed after Ce4+ oxidation with six water molecules per cerium nitrate hydrate, proposing slight excess water. No additional water was used; no water activity or dissolved coordination was measured.','mechanistic stoichiometry',[('hydroxyl count in proposed precipitation','4','formula_count'),('hydrate water count','6','formula_count')],'C5','author_interpretation'),
('fast-deprotonation','main',4,'Discussion','Proposed TEA role','Immediate yellow color and thickening to khaki yellow are attributed to simultaneous complex formation/deprotonation. Alcohols are considered less likely than water to participate; TEA hydrogen bonding/proton removal is proposed. Reaction reference27 has no supplied bibliography entry.','current-route mechanism',[],'C1 C5 C9 G8','author_interpretation'),
('packing-result','main',4,'Discussion final paragraph','Porous powder and density','Room-temperature-dried precipitates are porous with rough packing density~0.6g/cm³, compared with cited bulk density7.65g/cm³. The bulk value is not a measured density of these powders.','dried precipitate and literature reference',[('rough powder packing density','~0.6','g/cm3'),('cited bulk density','7.65','g/cm3')],'','reported_measurement_and_reference'),
('conclusion-dls','main',4,'Conclusions','Conclusion size terminology','Conclusion repeats3nm(SD1) crystallites and describes butanol14±8nm,propanol50±18nm,ethanol72±20nm as aggregate hydrodynamic diameters. This conflicts with Results radii.','three-solvent conclusion',[],'C2','reported_summary'),
('outlook','main',4,'Conclusions last sentences','Proposed future uses','Doped ceria electrodes and transition-metal-supported ceria water-gas-shift catalysts are proposed possibilities. Neither synthesis nor performance is demonstrated in these sources.','outlook',[],'G8','author_outlook'),
('acknowledgments','main',4,'Acknowledgment','Acknowledgments','DLS and XRD assistance, Army Research Laboratory contractW911QX-04-C-0105, NSFDMR-0080008 and Maryland NanoCenter/NispLab support are acknowledged.','paper context',[],'','bibliographic'),
('si-declaration','main',4,'Supporting Information Available','SI scope declaration','The main explicitly offers XPS spectra and detailed peak fitting procedures. Supplied4-page SI matches this content and the exact main oxidation-state results; no other SI is claimed.','paired document',[],'','source_identity'),
('si-peak-labels','si',1,'Calculation section continuedSIp2','Literal XPS labels','SI assigns u/v to Ce3d5/2 and3d7/2, respectively, as printed; 3d7/2 is physically atypical and retained as a source-label issue. CeIII uses v0,u0,v′,u′ and CeIV uses v,u,v″,u″,v‴,u‴.','XPS model labels',[],'C6','source_model'),
('si-exposure','si',2,'First paragraph and FigureS1','X-ray exposure contexts','FigureS1 is organized as(a)as-prepared long exposure,(b)as-prepared short,(c)calcined long,(d)calcined short, supported by SI first paragraph and spectra. Caption/fitting prose have inconsistent labels. Long exposure is more than5h; short exposure is~15min in caption and15min in prose.','four XPS spectra',[('long exposure','>5','h'),('SI caption short exposure','~15','min'),('SI prose short exposure','15','min')],'C4 C7','reported_acquisition'),
('si-fitting','si',2,'Fitting paragraph','Fitting framework','Apply Shirley background and50%Gaussian/50%Lorentzian peak shape. Calibrate to the printed v¹ component884.5eV; TableS1 v′ energy884.8eV is retained separately. Stronger long-exposure spectra supply fitting positions/widths for lower-SNR short-exposure data.','XPS fitting protocol',[('Gaussian fraction','50','%'),('Lorentzian fraction','50','%'),('printed calibration energy','884.5','eV')],'C7 C10','reported_analysis'),
('si-fit-four','si',2,'Fitting paragraph','Long-exposure as-prepared fit','FigureS1a is described as fitting four CeIII peaks; absence of an~916eV feature is interpreted as no CeIV evidence. It is not a measured exact zero bulk CeIV fraction.','as-prepared long-exposure surface',[('fit peak count','4','count'),('absent CeIV marker position','~916','eV')],'C7','reported_analysis'),
('si-fit-ten','si',2,'Fitting paragraph','Mixed-valence fit and inherited parameters','SI refers repeatedly to spectrum3a/3c, while actual SI figure isS1 and main Figure3 is thermal analysis. A10-peak mixed CeIII/CeIV fit supplies the TableS1 values used for remaining spectra. Preserve this label discrepancy rather than assigning thermal data as XPS.','XPS fitting context',[('mixed fit peak count','10','count')],'C7','reported_analysis'),
('si-short-results','si',3,'Paragraph after equations','SI short-exposure fractions','After~15min X-rays, SI reports as-prepared81%CeIII/19%CeIV and calcined33%CeIII/67%CeIV, matching main values but not its strict<15min wording.','short-exposure surfaces',[('SI short exposure','~15','min'),('as-prepared CeIII','81','%'),('as-prepared CeIV','19','%'),('calcined CeIII','33','%'),('calcined CeIV','67','%')],'C4 C8','reported_measurement'),
('si-long-results','si',3,'Final paragraph','Photoreduction with exposure','After more than5h, as-prepared material shows no evidence of CeIV; calcined sample CeIII increases to~45%. The unreported complementary percentage is not inserted as a measured value.','long-exposure surfaces',[('long exposure','>5','h'),('calcined CeIII after long exposure','~45','%')],'C7 C8','reported_measurement'),
]
CONFLICTS=[
('C1','TEA formula and reaction typography','Mainp1 prints(C2H5OH)3N although named triethanolamine; mainp4 reaction also lacks an opening reagent parenthesis. Preserve literal strings and named identity separately; no molecular graph is qualified here.'),
('C2','DLS radius versus diameter','Mainp2 calls72/50/14nm average hydrodynamic radii; p4 calls the same values diameters. Preserve the values and both definitions, without multiplying or dividing by2.'),
('C3','As-prepared product scope','TEM/SAED assigns CeO2 crystallites, but bulk XRD is unidentified and the authors propose a Ce–TEA/hydroxide/hydrated-ceria mixture. No whole as-prepared pure-CeO2 composition is established.'),
('C4','Short XPS exposure wording','Mainp3 reports less than15min; SIcaption/p3 approximately15min and SIp2 exactly15min wording. Keep separate source assertions.'),
('C5','Mechanistic complex inconsistency','Mainp3 expects no hydroxo/aquo complex from absent pinkish-purple color; p4 proposes simultaneous hydroxo and TEA complexes. Both remain author interpretations.'),
('C6','Printed spin-orbit labels','SI prose/table print Ce3d7/2 for v and3d5/2 for u. Do not silently substitute a conventional label.'),
('C7','SI panel and figure references','S1caption first clause lacks(a)/places(b) ambiguously. SI first paragraph placesa/b as-prepared andc/d calcined, but later saysa andc are as-prepared. Fitting prose/TableS1 cite3a/3c despite onlyS1 supplied.'),
('C8','CeIII area expression','SI CeIII equation prints I_v without prime, unlike its own CeIII peak assignment/table, and I_v also occurs in CeIV sum. Retain literal expression and do not recalculate source percentages from a silently repaired formula.'),
('C9','Missing reference27','Mainp4 cites27 for the proposed reaction; supplied main bibliography contains1–26 and28. Missing identity remains unresolved.'),
('C10','Calibration versus table','SI calibrates printed v¹ to884.5eV, while TableS1 lists v′884.8eV. Do not harmonize by editing either value.'),
]
GAPS=[
('G1','Stock and solution preparation','Masses, stock preparation volumes/storage and mixing settings are unreported. No numerical room-temperature setting or measured solution speciation is supplied.'),
('G2','Workup and diagnostic missingness','Wash volumes/cycles, filter vacuum, stirring speed, NH4OH concentration/amount and explicit control outcome are absent. No post-acetone drying details are supplied.'),
('G3','Calcination missingness','Calcination atmosphere, furnace/loading and cooling are unreported. TGA air must not be inherited by synthesis calcination.'),
('G4','Measurement/specimen joins','Several measurement settings, replicates and batch identities are absent. XRD/XPS solvent provenance is not explicit; Figure3buses butanol as-prepared, but exact calcined DSC physical batch is not uniquely established.'),
('G5','Atomic structure missingness','No supplied crystallographic coordinates, CIF, occupancy or numerical lattice parameter; local fringes/SAED/XRD do not establish a resolved atomic recipe pair.'),
('G6','Raw measurements','Spectral curves, DLS distributions and peak areas are not supplied as numeric raw arrays. Original plots are retained without guessed digitization.'),
('G7','Yield and purity','Isolated yield/product mass and quantitative as-prepared phase fractions are not reported; local crystallites, surface oxidation state and whole-powder composition remain different observables.'),
('G8','Cited-only context','Cited precursor/material/thermal and mechanism papers were not independently retrieved or read; missing reference27 cannot be reconstructed. Future doped/supported catalyst uses are not extra measured syntheses.'),
]
